{
  "enabled": true,
  "name": "KRIA Fleet Execution Guard",
  "description": "Protect KRIA fleet execution from substrate confusion and routing errors. Enforce strict distinction between host, VM, docker, and remote node execution contexts. Prevent host operations from being routed to incorrect substrates and preserve execution substrate discipline aligned with KRIA authoritative orchestration principles.",
  "version": "1",
  "when": {
    "type": "preToolUse",
    "toolTypes": [
      "execute_fleet_command"
    ]
  },
  "then": {
    "type": "askAgent",
    "prompt": "You are about to execute an execute_fleet_command tool call in KRIA.\n\nBefore proceeding, enforce substrate execution discipline and prevent routing errors:\n\nVALIDATE EXECUTION SUBSTRATE:\n1. Identify target substrate explicitly:\n   - HOST: Local machine, direct system access, privileged operations\n   - VM: Virtual machine instance, isolated environment, guest OS\n   - DOCKER: Container runtime, ephemeral, image-based execution\n   - REMOTE_NODE: Network-accessible node, SSH/API-based, distributed execution\n\n2. Verify substrate matches operation intent:\n   - Host operations (system config, kernel, privileged access) → HOST only\n   - Isolated workloads (testing, sandboxing) → VM or DOCKER\n   - Distributed operations (cluster, multi-node) → REMOTE_NODE\n   - Development/local work → HOST or DOCKER\n\nPREVENT SUBSTRATE CONFUSION:\n- Do NOT route host-level operations to VM/DOCKER/REMOTE_NODE\n- Do NOT route container operations to HOST directly\n- Do NOT route privileged operations to unprivileged substrates\n- Do NOT assume substrate from command content; require explicit specification\n\nDISTINGUISH EXECUTION CONTEXTS:\n- HOST: Direct filesystem access, system services, kernel operations, privileged commands\n- VM: Guest OS isolation, snapshot/rollback capability, resource limits, guest-level operations\n- DOCKER: Container isolation, image-based, ephemeral state, containerized services\n- REMOTE_NODE: Network-based, SSH/API access, distributed state, multi-node coordination\n\nVALIDATE COMMAND COMPATIBILITY:\n- Host operations (systemctl, modprobe, iptables, /etc modifications) → HOST only\n- Package management (apt, yum, brew) → Verify substrate supports package manager\n- Container operations (docker run, docker build) → HOST or REMOTE_NODE with docker daemon\n- VM operations (virsh, qemu, hypervisor commands) → HOST or REMOTE_NODE with hypervisor\n- Application-level commands → Any substrate with runtime support\n\nENFORCE ROUTING DISCIPLINE:\n- Explicit substrate parameter required in execute_fleet_command\n- No implicit substrate inference from command syntax\n- No substrate fallback chains (HOST → VM → DOCKER)\n- Substrate mismatch is a blocking error, not a warning\n\nPRESERVE AUTHORITATIVE ORCHESTRATION:\n- KRIA maintains execution substrate authority\n- Substrates are execution providers, not decision-makers\n- Routing decisions remain with orchestrator, not delegated to tools\n- Preserve bounded cognition: validate substrate once, route decisively\n\nDETECT DANGEROUS PATTERNS:\n- Host operations without explicit HOST substrate specification\n- Privileged operations routed to unprivileged substrates\n- Cross-substrate assumptions (e.g., \"docker run\" on non-docker substrate)\n- Substrate confusion in multi-node execution\n\nIf substrate is explicitly specified and matches operation intent, proceed with execute_fleet_command immediately.\n\nIf substrate is ambiguous, missing, or mismatched with operation intent, briefly state the substrate confusion and require explicit substrate specification before proceeding."
  }
}